From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:44:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the Microsoft Surface Laptop 13-inch

Add a device tree for the Surface Laptop 13-inch 1st Edition with
Snapdragon (model 2095, X1P-42-100, Purwa).

No tree for it is posted. An owner keeps a working one as an edited
decompiled DTB (kairunjp/Surface-Laptop-for-Business-13in-1st-Ed-with-
Snapdragon-Linux at 3d52bdac42), with the display, USB-C and USB-A
ports, keyboard, touchpad, UFS, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, battery and
touchscreen working, and notes from the laptop's ACPI tables. Where it
names a pin, the pin is the Surface Pro 12-inch's: the panel power
switch (TLMM 29), the WCN7850's 3.3 V switch (TLMM 214) and its Wi-Fi
and Bluetooth enables (TLMM 117 and 116), PCIe4's PERST and WAKE (TLMM
146 and 148), the touchscreen's interrupt on i2c8 (TLMM 38), Bluetooth
on uart14 (ACPI BTH0 on UR15), the reserved GPIOs (34-35, 44-47) and
the two WSA8845 speakers on the WSA SoundWire bus. So the tree is the
Surface Pro 12-inch's, with its regulators, and differs where the
laptop does:

- The touchscreen is ACPI MSHW0468 at 0x34 on i2c8, at 100 kHz as in
  the owner's tree. Its _DSM gives HID descriptor register 0; register
  1 reads back an all-zero descriptor.
- The multiport USB controller serves the USB-A port and the internal
  keyboard and touchpad. Its PHY supplies are the X1 CRD's.
- The panel is powered through TLMM 29 alone, as in the owner's tree.
  panel-edp has no entry for its LGD 0x07ad and uses its conservative
  timings.
- The DSP and GPU firmware load from qcom/x1p42100/Microsoft/
  SurfaceLaptop13. Microsoft's driver package for this laptop carries
  an audio DSP image and adsp_dtbs.elf of its own; the compute DSP and
  GPU files are the Surface Pro 12-inch's.
- The sound card keeps the Surface Pro 12-inch's name, which is the
  name of the topology linux-firmware ships for it. The speakers reset
  through LPASS TLMM 12, as on every Surface X1 tree, where the owner's
  tree uses TLMM 204 and 205 and its speakers work only partly.

The volume keys, the type cover's hall sensor and the cameras are the
tablet's. The Surface Aggregator Module, the lid sensor, the USB-C SBU
muxes and the I2C buses the Surface Pro 12-inch enables without devices
are left out until the laptop's ACPI tables show them, so displays over
USB-C are not described yet.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
